{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "tanstack-start-app",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-09-24",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "main": "@tanstack/react-start/server-entry",

  /* Smart Placement — run the Worker near our BACKENDS, not near the
   * visitor. Beka 2026-10-13, from error_logs:
   *
   *   attractions · 403  [anthropic] 403 Forbidden [edge:HKG]
   *   guide       · 403  [anthropic] 403 Forbidden [edge:HKG]
   *
   * Anthropic does not serve the API from Hong Kong, so when
   * Cloudflare happens to run us in its HKG datacentre every Claude
   * call comes back "Request not allowed" and the visitor sees "AI is
   * temporarily busy". All four occurrences on record are HKG; no
   * other edge has ever produced it.
   *
   * By default a Worker executes at the edge nearest the VISITOR,
   * which is what puts us in HKG for anyone in that region. Smart
   * Placement instead watches where our subrequests actually go —
   * Anthropic (US), Supabase (Frankfurt), Azure Speech and Blob
   * (Germany), Google Places — and relocates execution near that
   * centre of gravity. Our handlers are backend-heavy (several
   * round-trips per request, some of them 40 s Claude calls), so this
   * is the case Cloudflare designed the feature for: total latency
   * usually drops too, because we trade one slow user hop for several
   * fast backend hops.
   *
   * HONEST LIMIT: this optimises for backend latency, not for dodging
   * a geo-block. It makes HKG execution very unlikely, not
   * impossible. If the 403 ever reappears in error_logs after this,
   * the deterministic fix is to make the Anthropic call from a
   * Durable Object pinned with locationHint "weur"/"enam" — more
   * machinery, so it is not worth it until the cheap fix is proven
   * insufficient.
   */
  "placement": { "mode": "smart" },
}
